@* Table's Overview, written by hand after Button's: the URL-first behavior (sorting, paging,
   filters) previewed in each mode, selection in a form, then state in the component (Live only),
   data from a database, row links and the look as code. From docs/components.md (Table) and the
   dev host's TableDemo. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Sorting and paging</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Sortable</Code> on a column and <Code>PageSize</Code> on the table. Headers and page numbers are links that set <Code>?sort=</Code> and <Code>?page=</Code>, so the table works with no script, survives a reload and can be shared.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-sort-page" />
<TextBlock><Code>InitialSort</Code> sets the order before anyone picks one (<Code>"-date"</Code> for newest first), and <Code>PageSizeOptions</Code> adds a "Rows per page" menu.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Column filters</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Filterable</Code> on a column for a filter in its header. Text matches anywhere, a date its day, enums and booleans get a menu, and a number takes a range: at least, at most, or both.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-filters" />
<TextBlock>Filters go in the URL as <Code>?f-customer=ada</Code> (here <Code>?orders-f-customer=ada</Code>: this table has a <Code>QueryPrefix</Code>, so it sorts and pages apart from the one above, as under <Anchor Href="docs/components/table#several-tables-on-a-page">Several tables on a page</Anchor>). A number's two ends go in as <Code>?f-total-min=100&amp;f-total-max=500</Code>, so the form needs no script. <Code>FilterMultiple</Code> lets an enum column match several values. For filters of your own, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor> above the table.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Selection</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Selectable</Code> with an <Code>ItemKey</Code> for a checkbox per row. In a form, the checked rows post their keys, so this works with JavaScript off.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-selection" />
<TextBlock>Read the keys with <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm(Name = "selected")]</Code> on a static page, or <Code>@@bind-SelectedKeys</Code> on an interactive one. Give columns <Code>FooterText</Code> for a totals row.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">State in the component</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>@@bind-Sort</Code>, <Code>@@bind-Page</Code> and <Code>@@bind-Filters</Code> on an interactive page to keep them in C# instead of the URL: for a table in a dialog, or anywhere the address shouldn't change.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-bound" />
<TextBlock>The table starts from the URL if it has values, and a new sort or filter goes back to page 1. Set the page back to 1 yourself when your own filters change.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Data from a database</Heading>
<TextBlock>Pass an EF Core <Code>IQueryable</Code> as <Code>Items</Code>, and the database sorts, filters and pages. For more control, use <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> to load each page yourself:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Provider</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Paging without a total</Heading>
<TextBlock>When the data pages by cursor (an opaque "next" token, as many APIs and logs do) or has no total, set <Code>Paging</Code> and return the next cursor from your <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>. The table passes it back as <Code>request.Cursor</Code>; the total is optional.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Cursor</Code>
<TextBlock><Code>TablePaging.LoadMore</Code> puts a button under the rows that adds the next page to them; <Code>LoadMoreText</Code> sets its text.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-more" />
<TextBlock>On static pages and with JavaScript off there's nothing to add rows to, so the button shows the next page in place of this one, and First goes back to the start. With an <Code>ItemKey</Code>, a row already shown isn't added twice. A parent's re-render keeps the added pages; call <Code>ReloadAsync()</Code> to start over.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Code>TablePaging.NextPrevious</Code> shows First, Previous and Next. Return <Code>Previous</Code> too if your source has it; without it, interactive tables go back through the cursors they've followed, and static ones offer First.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-cursor-pages" />
<TextBlock><Code>TablePaging.LoadOnScroll</Code> loads the next page as the last row comes into view, on interactive pages. Give the table a <Code>MaxHeight</Code>, so it scrolls in its own box and what's under it stays within reach. Its Load more button is for the keyboard, out of sight until it has focus; on static pages and with JavaScript off it shows, as a link to the next page. Both modes also work with <Code>Items</Code>, where the table pages by row offset.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-on-scroll" />

<Heading Level="2">Row links</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>RowHref</Code> to make each row a link, and <Code>RowLabel</Code> to name it. Links, buttons and checkboxes inside the row still work on their own. Use <Code>RowCurrent</Code> to mark the row the page is showing, such as the one whose details are beside the table: it's drawn as selected and screen readers hear it as current (<Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor> has one with its details in an aside).</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@RowLinks</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Several tables on a page</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>QueryPrefix</Code> to give each table its own keys in the URL, so they sort and page on their own.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Prefix</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Lines, size and scrolling</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Size</Code>, <Code>Striped</Code>, <Code>ColumnLines</Code>, <Code>RowLines</Code> and <Code>Bordered</Code> for the look; turn <Code>Bordered</Code> off for a table in a card. <Code>MaxHeight</Code> scrolls the rows under a sticky header, and <Code>EmptyText</Code> shows when there are none.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Look</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<Heading Level="2">See also</Heading>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor>: search and filters of your own, in the URL.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/pagination">Pagination</Anchor>: page links for a list that isn't a table.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: state in the URL on static pages.</ListItem>
</List>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Table Items="orders" PageSize="10" Caption="Orders">
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Customer" Sortable Filterable />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Total" Format="C" Sortable />
            <TemplateColumn Title="Status">
                <Badge>@context.Status</Badge>
            </TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        """;

    private const string Provider = """
        <Table TItem="Order" ItemsProvider="LoadOrders" PageSize="10">...</Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<TableResult<Order>> LoadOrders(TableRequest<Order> request)
            {
                var query = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Active);
                var page = await request.Apply(query).ToListAsync();   // filters, sorts, pages
                return new(page, await request.ApplyFiltering(query).CountAsync());
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string Cursor = """
        <Table TItem="AuditEvent" ItemsProvider="LoadEvents" PageSize="200" Paging="TablePaging.LoadMore">...</Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<TableResult<AuditEvent>> LoadEvents(TableRequest<AuditEvent> request)
            {
                var page = await api.ListEventsAsync(cursor: request.Cursor, limit: request.PageSize ?? 200);
                return new(page.Items) { Next = page.Next };   // no total needed
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string RowLinks = """
        <Table Items="orders" RowHref='o => $"orders/{o.Id}"' RowLabel='o => $"order {o.Number}"'>...</Table>
        """;

    private const string Prefix = """
        <Table Items="orders" QueryPrefix="o-" PageSize="10">...</Table>     @* ?o-sort=, ?o-page= *@
        <Table Items="refunds" QueryPrefix="r-" PageSize="10">...</Table>
        """;

    private const string Look = """
        <Table Items="orders" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Striped ColumnLines>...</Table>
        <Table Items="orders" MaxHeight="Length.Rem(20)" EmptyText="No orders yet">...</Table>
        """;
}
